plicit adaptive choices and separately stored\nREADME.md:69:confirmation in the active browser, then keeps the same response-free run open\nREADME.md:77:action first settle the active Verona draft; saving modes require an accessible\nREADME.md:98:interactive test surface with a completion summary, so no Verona/fallback\nREADME.md:100:control remains available. This is production-browser-gated for both normal\nREADME.md:141:with the saved roster during runtime refresh, including in a different browser\nREADME.md:159:protected browser flow carries one original `monitor-group` login through\nREADME.md:229:- active demo content release with a three-unit, Verona API 6-backed demo booklet\nREADME.md:233:and reload restoration path as imported production Verona packages. It does not\nREADME.md:256:For a quicker pre-push signal that builds once, reuses built artifacts, includes the SQLite runtime preflight, and stops the browser flow after the content import/activation read model, run:\nREADME.md:267:npm run ci:browser:quick\nREADME.md:268:npm run ci:browser:review\nREADME.md:269:npm run ci:browser:monitor\nREADME.md:270:npm run ci:browser:ops\nREADME.md:274:The browser groups build once and then reuse built artifacts: `ci:browser:quick` covers content, roster links, participant entry, and activation roster warnings; `ci:browser:review` covers response/review handoff paths; `ci:browser:monitor` covers confirmed monitor batch commands, open-run synchronization, and activation-blocking runtime handoff; `ci:browser:ops` covers destructive operator actions and protected-operator auth.\nREADME.md:287:- browser-driven Angular UI smokes against SQLite in focused content, participant-entry, activation-roster-warning, review-readiness, open-run, monitor-detail, destructive-action, full, and protected operator modes, plus a Postgres-backed protected UI smoke in the Postgres CI matrix\nREADME.md:305:Before starting a built runtime, you can run a deployability preflight that verifies compiled API/store artifacts, the Angular browser bundle, the frontend index-to-asset references, optional build metadata, and storage readiness:\nREADME.md:323:Optional browser-computed proof of work can protect the three Original Testcenter credential boundaries independently. Set `FIRST_SLICE_PROOF_OF_WORK_SCOPES` to a comma- or whitespace-separated subset of `admin`, `participant`, and `second_code`; production deployments should normally enable all three. The admin and participant scopes protect every non-empty login identity, including empty-password attempts and passwordless roster entries, so omitting a required password cannot bypass the work factor and cheaply fill a login failure sink. Any enabled scope requires a server-only `FIRST_SLICE_PROOF_OF_WORK_SECRET` of at least 32 characters. `FIRST_SLICE_PROOF_OF_WORK_MAX_NUMBER` controls the SHA-256 search range from 1 through 10,000,000 (default `1000000`), and `FIRST_SLICE_PROOF_OF_WORK_TTL_MS` controls challenge lifetime from 1,000 through 600,000 milliseconds (default `120000`). With no enabled scopes, the existing sign-in contracts remain compatible and no challenge is computed.\nREADME.md:480:`FIRST_SLICE_MAX_JSON_BODY_BYTES` defaults to `1048576` and limits ordinary JSON command payloads before they are accumulated in memory. Source-package create, replacement, and retry commands use the separate `FIRST_SLICE_MAX_SOURCE_PACKAGE_JSON_BODY_BYTES` limit, which defaults to `75497472` so the documented 50 MiB extracted-package ceiling plus base64 overhead remains reachable without weakening every API route. Participant `save-progress` requests independently use `FIRST_SLICE_MAX_PARTICIPANT_PROGRESS_JSON_BODY_BYTES`, defaulting to `67108864`, so large Verona/GeoGebra state remains durable without widening administrator and other ordinary command routes. Oversized requests return `413 request_body_too_large` with the applicable route limit.\nREADME.md:610:The System Check network stage now mirrors the Original browser-profile\nREADME.md:633:Original review surface: it lists every captured computer/browser, network, and\nREADME.md:635:styling onto affected entries. The anonymous and dedicated-login browser gates\nREADME.md:638:Verona results now use the Original `ResponsesForSysCheck` payload as well:\nREADME.md:647:configured network measurement, Verona Unit/Player, questionnaire, and report.\nREADME.md:668:choices: `Bericht senden` and `System-Check abbrechen`. The latter returns\nREADME.md:679:- the Angular shell derives the active operator access mode from the authenticated session and retains the session read model across reloads alongside the existing token state; monitor-only accounts receive a reduced Monitor plus Access & Diagnostics navigation, a focused open-run command console, scoped SSE/polling refresh, and no workspace/content/admin-management controls. Protected monitor commands bind their actor to the authenticated account instead of trusting request metadata and emit the Original TestLog events `command executed` plus `locked by monitor` with the original command spelling. A protected browser gate signs in as an actual group monitor and verifies that unrelated group runs are absent\nREADME.md:680:- the Original central error-report flow is available for participant and operator surfaces: application request failures offer a report action and unhandled Angular/browser errors open the same accessible dialog automatically. The sanitized report contains an error ID, timestamp, build, browser/device, parameter-free URL, stack, and a bounded previous-error buffer and can be inspected, copied, or downloaded as `bug-report.txt`. Optional direct GitHub issue delivery is enabled only when both `BUG_REPORT_GITHUB_REPOSITORY` and the server-only `BUG_REPORT_GITHUB_TOKEN` are configured; delivery is rate-limited and timeout-bounded, while shared browser/server redaction removes common credentials and tokens before submission. The dedicated `smoke:ui:bug-report` production SQLite/Chromium gate triggers the global handler, verifies redaction, and downloads the report\nREADME.md:704:- participant roster import now persists operator-managed login/stable-group-key/authored-group-label/booklet/display-name rows from CSV/TSV/semicolon text with canonical or common alias headers, optional password/`pw` fields, Testtaker/Participant-style XML, original Testcenter `Testtakers`/`Login` XML, JSON roster text, or native JSON request objects/arrays, including nested Group/Booklet parent contexts, every ordered `<Login><Booklet>` assignment, original comma-separated per-booklet `state=\"key:option, key:option\"` adaptive presets, original `<Booklet codes=\"…\">` second-code mappings, and file-level `CustomTexts`; native JSON accepts inherited maps, value objects, and key/text entry arrays with participant-level overrides. Presets are checked against the active release, shown in Angular roster cards, copied durably into new runs, and remain authoritative in participant and monitor routing, while the exact current 41-key participant text set reaches login, Starter, and active Player controls including code-error and Starter-action labels, Verona loading/failure guidance and the fixed Original reload action, the page-navigation prompt and five-second timer start/expiry/cancellation notices. `%s` placeholders are substituted safely, imported mixed-case block codes remain usable with the original uppercase dialog behavior, and protected participant passwords are hashed internally with roster CSV exposing only `passwordRequired`\nREADME.md:724:- original `<Booklet codes=\"alpha beta\">` assignments now trigger a password-first second-code challenge in participant sign-in and starter launch; a valid code exposes its matching assignments together with uncoded assignments, different codes create or resume distinct durable sessions, the Angular prompt appears only when required, and configured alternative codes are not returned in participant roster context or persisted in browser storage\nREADME.md:730:- participant current-state can now deliver imported Verona player HTML and exact inline or referenced unit definitions; the Angular participant route runs supported Verona 2–6 players in a sandboxed frame, exposes accessible queued, indeterminate-loading, and exact 100%-loaded host milestones with original per-login custom texts, exchanges start/state/navigation/runtime-error messages, merges separately reported unit/player state, persists the versioned envelope through coalescing autosave with visible retry, and restores it after session reload. Loading progress stays truthful to the in-browser `srcdoc` lifecycle instead of fabricating byte percentages for Player HTML and Unit definitions already resident in current state. A typed in-memory state avoids repeatedly parsing production-sized definitions, while foreground navigation pauses eager autosave and keeps separate Prev/Next controls independent from the optional unit menu. Browser smoke records the exact loading-phase order and executes the pinned original Verona 6 adaptive sample, the provenance-pinned official `verona-player-simple` 1.0.1/API-2, 2.1.0/API-3, 4.0.0/API-4, and 5.2.0/API-5 releases with their generation-specific state restoration, the independent official ABI 3.3.0 scripted-survey Player/API-2.1 and current ABI 5.0.0 metadata-spec-5/runtime-API-4 release with text and radio answer restoration, the independent official EVA 1.0.0 scripted-survey Player/API-2.1 with its narrowly corrected historical Ready declaration and text-answer restoration, the independent official DAN 3.0.0 visual-assessment Player/API-2.1 and current DAN 3.1.0 metadata-spec-5/runtime-API-4 release with positioned multiline-text and multiple-choice restoration, the historical official Speedtest 1.2.0 Player with a mixed metadata-spec-5/runtime-API-4 declaration plus timed-choice restoration, the current official Speedtest 3.3.0 Player/API-5.2 with multi-part choice-state 