{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T19:21:49.787Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a04ef8-97db-7fc1-ac95-5f0613e4f05a","call_id":"call_pHnpglZItWiTZfh7USZ8NzqR","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.8 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":" (https://docs.entire.io/llms.txt)\nciteturn30view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/plain; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.entire.io/llms.txt\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 119\nL0: # Entire\nL1: - [Entire Documentation](https://docs.entire.io/index.md): Capture, search, and review the context behind agent work.\nL2: - [Introduction](https://docs.entire.io/overview.md): Git shows what changed. Entire shows why.\nL3: - [Quickstart](https://docs.entire.io/quickstart.md): Get started with Entire by creating your first checkpoint.\nL4: - [Installation](https://docs.entire.io/installation.md): Install, update, verify, and uninstall the Entire CLI.\nL5: - [entire.io](https://docs.entire.io/platforms/entire-io.md): Review, search, and share the agent history Entire captured.\nL6: - [Overview](https://docs.entire.io/guides/checkpoints/overview.md): Learn what checkpoints are, why they matter, and how Entire captures them.\nL7: - [Capture Checkpoints](https://docs.entire.io/guides/checkpoints/capture-checkpoints.md): Learn how Entire turns agent work into committed checkpoints.\nL8: - [Checkpoint Storage](https://docs.entire.io/guides/checkpoints/checkpoint-storage.md): Where Entire keeps checkpoints, which backend a repository uses, and how the two backends coexist.\nL9: - [Store Checkpoints in Another Repository](https://docs.entire.io/guides/checkpoints/store-checkpoints-in-another-repo.md): Push checkpoint history to a repository separate from your source code.\nL10: - [Keep Checkpoints Local](https://docs.entire.io/guides/checkpoints/keep-checkpoints-local.md): Capture checkpoint history without pushing it to a remote repository.\nL11: - [Inspect Checkpoints](https://docs.entire.io/guides/checkpoints/inspect-checkpoints.md): Choose how to inspect checkpoint context in entire.io or the Entire CLI.\nL12: - [Inspect Checkpoints in entire.io](https://docs.entire.io/guides/checkpoints/inspect-in-entire-io.md): Open checkpoint details, sessions, and file diffs in entire.io.\nL13: - [Inspect Checkpoints in CLI](https://docs.entire.io/guides/checkpoints/inspect-in-cli.md): List, explain, and measure checkpoints from a terminal.\nL14: - [Troubleshooting](https://docs.entire.io/guides/checkpoints/troubleshooting.md): Fix common checkpoint capture, linking, and sync issues.\nL15: - [Overview](https://docs.entire.io/guides/repositories/overview.md): Learn what Entire Repositories are, how they work, and why they matter for agent-driven development.\nL16: - [Install the Entire GitHub App](https://docs.entire.io/guides/repositories/github-app-access.md): Authorize the Entire GitHub App and choose which repositories Entire can access.\nL17: - [Mirrors](https://docs.entire.io/guides/repositories/mirrors.md): Connect an existing GitHub repository to Entire with an Entire-backed Git remote.\nL18: - [Mirror Repositories in entire.io](https://docs.entire.io/guides/repositories/mirrors-in-entire-io.md): Configure GitHub access, choose repositories, and create mirrors from entire.io.\nL19: - [Mirror Repositories in the CLI](https://docs.entire.io/guides/repositories/mirrors-in-cli.md): Create GitHub repository mirrors with the Entire CLI and configure Git remotes.\nL20: - [Mirror Access and Permissions](https://docs.entire.io/guides/repositories/mirror-access-and-permissions.md): Understand how repository mirror access follows GitHub collaborator permissions.\nL21: - [Troubleshooting](https://docs.entire.io/guides/repositories/troubleshooting.md): Fix common repository mirror setup, access, clone, fetch, and push issues.\nL22: - [Overview](https://docs.entire.io/guides/sessions/overview.md): Understand agent sessions, what they capture, and how they connect to checkpoints.\nL23: - [Sessions in entire.io](https://docs.entire.io/guides/sessions/sessions-in-entire-io.md): Review session timelines, transcripts, tool calls, files, and attribution in entire.io.\nL24: - [List Sessions](https://docs.entire.io/guides/sessions/list-sessions.md): Find active and ended sessions from the CLI.\nL25: - [Inspect Sessions in CLI](https://docs.entire.io/guides/sessions/inspect-sessions-in-cli.md): Inspect session metadata, transcript output, and token usage from the CLI.\nL26: - [Stop Sessions](https://docs.entire.io/guides/sessions/stop-sessions.md): End stale or active sessions from the CLI.\nL27: - [Attach Sessions](https://docs.entire.io/guides/sessions/attach-sessions.md): Link an existing agent session to a commit and checkpoint.\nL28: - [Resume Sessions](https://docs.entire.io/guides/sessions/resume-sessions.md): Continue previous agent work from a stopped session or branch.\nL29: - [Import Past Sessions](https://docs.entire.io/guides/sessions/import-past-agent-history.md): Bring past local agent sessions into Entire as read-only checkpoints.\nL30: - [Troubleshooting](https://docs.entire.io/guides/sessions/troubleshooting.md): Resolve missing, stale, unlinked, or misplaced sessions.\nL31: - [Overview](https://docs.entire.io/guides/graph/overview.md): entire-graph, a plugin for the Entire CLI that helps your agents find their answers fast and saves token spend by up to 71%.\nL32: - [Install and Enable entire-graph](https://docs.entire.io/guides/graph/set-up-graph.md): Install entire-graph on your machine, add its instructions to a repository, and verify your coding agent can use it.\nL33: - [Use entire-graph with Your Coding Agent](https://docs.entire.io/guides/graph/use-graph.md): Ask your coding agent to use entire-graph to find code, trace relationships, and understand what a change could affect.\nL34: - [How entire-graph Works](https://docs.entire.io/guides/graph/how-graph-works.md): Understand how entire-graph analyzes a repository, guides your coding agent, and works with Search.\nL35: - [entire-graph Troubleshooting](https://docs.entire.io/guides/graph/troubleshooting.md): Fix entire-graph install, activation, adoption, cache, and coverage problems.\nL36: - [Overview](https://docs.entire.io/guides/search/overview.md): Use search to find past agent work and its context, and to locate relevant code snippets and other text across your repos.\nL37: - [Search in entire.io](https://docs.entire.io/guides/search/search-in-entire-io.md): Find past agent work and indexed source code from your browser.\nL38: - [Search in the CLI](https://docs.entire.io/guides/search/search-in-cli.md): Search past agent work or indexed source code from the Entire CLI.\nL39: - [Search from Your Agent](https://docs.entire.io/guides/search/search-from-your-agent.md): Use the search Skill so your agent can find past work or text in indexed source files.\nL40: - [What Is Indexed and When](https://docs.entire.io/guides/search/what-is-indexed.md): Understand what Search can find, when new content appears, and how repository scope affects results.\nL41: - [Search Troubleshooting](https://docs.entire.io/guides/search/troubleshooting.md): Fix common history and code search issues in the CLI, entire.io, and the search Skill.\nL42: - [Dispatches](https://docs.entire.io/guides/dispatches/overview.md): Summarize recent agent work into a shareable markdown update.\nL43: - [Dispatches in entire.io](https://docs.entire.io/guides/dispatches/dispatches-in-entire-io.md): Generate and preview Dispatches from the Entire web interface.\nL44: - [Dispatches in CLI](https://docs.entire.io/guides/dispatches/dispatches-in-cli.md): Use `entire dispatch` to generate markdown summaries from the terminal.\nL45: - [Troubleshooting](https://docs.entire.io/guides/dispatches/troubleshooting.md): Fix common Dispatches visibility, scope, output, and CLI mode issues.\nL46: - [Configuration](https://docs.entire.io/guides/configuration/overview.md): Find the right place to configure Entire.\nL47: - [Local, Project, and Global Settings](https://docs.entire.io/guides/configuration/settings-layers.md): Configure shared repository behavior, personal overrides, and the files Entire writes under .entire.\nL48: - [Entire-Generated Files](https://docs.entire.io/guides/configuration/entire-files.md): Decide which Entire-generated files belong in Git and which should stay on your machine.\nL49: - [Git Hooks](https://docs.entire.io/guides/configuration/git-hooks.md): Configure the Git hooks Entire uses to capture and sync checkpoint data.\nL50: - [Privacy and Redaction](https://docs.entire.io/guides/configuration/privacy-and-redaction.md): Configure redaction layers, image externalization, telemetry, and checkpoint commit signing.\nL51: - [Logging and Diagnostics](https://docs.entire.io/guides/configuration/logging-and-diagnostics.md): Configure log verbosity, inspect active settings, and collect diagnostics.\nL52: - [Review Profiles](https://docs.entire.io/guides/configuration/review-profiles.md): Configure saved review profiles for entire review.\nL53: - [Security & Privacy](https://docs.entire.io/security.md): Where your data lives, which redaction layers run before each write, when data leaves your machine, and how to report security issues\nL54: - [Frequently Asked Questions](https://docs.entire.io/faq.md): Answers to common questions about checkpoints and attribution.\nL55: - [Glossary](https://docs.entire.io/glossary.md): The vocabulary Entire uses across the CLI, the web interface, and this documentation.\nL56: - [Delete Your Account](https://docs.entire.io/delete-account.md): Permanently delete your Entire account, and cancel during the 7-day cooldown\nL57: - [Agent Integrations](https://docs.entire.io/agents/overview.md): Compare Entire's built-in AI coding agent integrations and their capabilities\nL58: - [Set Up an Agent](https://docs.entire.io/agents/setup.md): Install, verify, update, and remove Entire agent hooks\nL59: - [Claude Code](https://docs.entire.io/agents/claude-code.md): Capture Claude Code transcripts, file changes, tool calls, tokens, and subagent sessions with Entire\nL60: - [Codex](https://docs.entire.io/agents/codex.md): Capture Codex transcripts, file changes, tokens, and subagent sessions with Entire\nL61: - [Copilot CLI](https://docs.entire.io/agents/copilot-cli.md): Capture Copilot CLI transcripts, file changes, tool calls, and token usage with Entire\nL62: - [Cursor](https://docs.entire.io/agents/cursor.md): Capture Cursor IDE and CLI transcripts, file changes, tool calls, and subagent sessions with Entire\nL63: - [Factory](https://docs.entire.io/agents/factory-droid.md): Capture Factory Droid transcripts, file changes, tool calls, tokens, and subagent sessions with Entire\nL64: - [Gemini CLI](https://docs.entire.io/agents/gemini-cli.md): Capture Gemini CLI transcripts, file changes, tool calls, and available token usage with Entire\nL65: - [OpenCode](https://docs.entire.io/agents/opencode.md): Capture OpenCode transcripts, file changes, tool calls, and token usage with Entire\nL66: - [Pi](https://docs.entire.io/agents/pi.md): Capture Pi transcripts, file changes, tool calls, and token usage with Entire\nL67: - [Agent Integration Protocol](https://docs.entire.io/agents/agent-integration-protocol/overview.md): Add support for an AI coding agent that Entire does not ship with\nL68: - [Build an Agent Integration](https://docs.entire.io/agents/agent-integration-protocol/build-an-agent-integration.md): Use the external-agents agent skill to add support for another coding agent\nL69: - [Architecture](https://docs.entire.io/agents/agent-integration-protocol/architecture.md): How the Entire CLI discovers and communicates with agent integrations\nL70: - [Lifecycle](https://docs.entire.io/agents/agent-integration-protocol/lifecycle.md): When the Entire CLI calls each agent integration command\nL71: - [Commands](https://docs.entire.io/agents/agent-integration-protocol/commands.md): Required commands and optional capabilities for agent integrations\nL72: - [Data Model](https://docs.entire.io/agents/agent-integration-protocol/data-model.md): Data types exchanged between the Entire CLI and agent integrations\nL73: - [activity](https://docs.entire.io/cli-reference/activity.md): Show your Entire activity overview from your hosted Entire account.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQuickstart - Entire (https://docs.entire.io/quickstart)\nciteturn30view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn29search4\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 262\nL0: > ## Documentation Index\nL1: >\nL2: > Fetch the complete documentation index at: cite0†/llms.txt L3: >\nL4: > Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\nL5: \nL6: cite1†Skip to main content L7: \nL8: cite2†Entire home page L9: \nL10: Search...\nL11: \nL12: Navigation\nL13: \nL14: Getting Started\nL15: \nL16: Quickstart\nL17: \nL18:   * cite3†GitHub†github.com L19:   * cite4†Discord†discord.gg L20:   * cite5†Blog†entire.io L21: \nL22: ### Getting Started\nL23: \nL24:   * cite2†Overview L25:   * cite6†Introduction L26:   * cite7†Quickstart L27:   * cite8†Installation L28:   * cite9†entire.io L29: ### Guides\nL30: \nL31:   * Checkpoints\nL32: \nL33:   * Repositories\nL34: \nL35:   * Sessions\nL36: \nL37:   * entire-graph\nL38: \nL39:   * Search\nL40: \nL41:   * Dispatches\nL42: \nL43:   * Configuration\nL44: \nL45: ### Security\nL46: \nL47:   * cite10†Security & Privacy L48: \nL49: ### Resources\nL50: \nL51:   * cite11†FAQ L52:   * cite12†Glossary L53:   * cite13†Delete Your Account L54: ## On this page\nL55: \nL56:   * cite14†Prerequisites L57:   * cite15†1. Install Entire L58:   * cite16†2. Create a Demo Project L59:   * cite17†3. Enable Entire L60:   * cite18†4. Create Your First Checkpoint L61:     * cite19†Start an Agent Session L62:     * cite20†Commit the Change L63:   * cite21†5. View Your First Checkpoint L64: \nL65: Getting Started\nL66: # Quickstart\nL67: \nL68: Get started with Entire by creating your first checkpoint.\nL69: \nL70: Copy page Copy page\nL71: \nL72: This quickstart walks you from installing Entire to creating your first checkpoint: a record of the prompts, transcript, tool calls, and file changes behind an AI-assisted commit.\nL73: ##\nL74: \nL75: cite14†​ L76: \nL77: Prerequisites\nL78: \nL79:   * Git installed and configured\nL80:   * An AI coding agent installed, such as Claude Code, Codex, Copilot CLI, Cursor, Factory, Gemini CLI, OpenCode, or Pi\nL81:   * macOS, Linux, or Windows\nL82:   * Optional: GitHub CLI (`gh`) installed and authenticated, if you want Entire to create and push a private GitHub repository during setup\nL83: ##\nL84: \nL85: cite15†​ L86: \nL87: 1. Install Entire\nL88: \nL89: Entire ships two release channels:\nL90: \nL91:   * `stable`: recommended for most users.\nL92:   * `nightly`: prerelease builds with newer changes.\nL93: Nightly is available through Homebrew and the Linux install script. Scoop currently supports stable only.\nL94: \nL95:   * macOS (Homebrew)\nL96: \nL97:   * Linux\nL98: \nL99:   * Windows (Scoop)\nL100:   * From Source\nL101: \nL102:     `# One-time setup: tap and trust\nL103:     brew tap entireio/tap\nL104:     brew trust entireio/tap\nL105: \nL106:     # Stable\nL107:     brew install --cask entire\nL108: \nL109:     # Nightly\nL110:     brew install --cask entire@nightly\nL111:     `\nL112: \nL113:     `# Stable\nL114:     curl -fsSL https://entire.io/install.sh | bash\nL115: \nL116:     # Nightly\nL117:     curl -fsSL https://entire.io/install.sh | bash -s -- --channel nightly\nL118:     `\nL119: \nL120:     `scoop bucket add entire https://github.com/entireio/scoop-bucket.git\nL121:     scoop install entire/entire\nL122:     `\nL123: \nL124:     `go install github.com/entireio/cli/cmd/entire@latest\nL125:     go install github.com/entireio/cli/cmd/git-remote-entire@latest\nL126:     `\nL127: Verify the installation:\nL128: \nL129:     `entire version\nL130:     `\nL131: ##\nL132: \nL133: cite16†​ L134: \nL135: 2. Create a Demo Project\nL136: \nL137:     `mkdir entire-demo\nL138:     cd entire-demo\nL139:     `\nL140: \nL141: Already in a Git repository? Skip this step and run the remaining commands from the repository root.\nL142: ##\nL143: \nL144: cite17†​ L145: \nL146: 3. Enable Entire\nL147: \nL148: Choose the agent you already use, then run its setup command.\nL149: \nL150: Claude Code\nL151: \nL152:     `entire enable -y --agent claude-code\nL153:     `\nL154: \nL155: Codex\nL156: \nL157:     `entire enable -y --agent codex\nL158:     `\nL159: \nL160: Copilot CLI\nL161: \nL162:     `entire enable -y --agent copilot-cli\nL163:     `\nL164: \nL165: Cursor\nL166: \nL167:     `entire enable -y --agent cursor\nL168:     `\nL169: \nL170: Factory\nL171: \nL172:     `entire enable -y --agent factoryai-droid\nL173:     `\nL174: \nL175: Gemini CLI\nL176: \nL177:     `entire enable -y --agent gemini\nL178:     `\nL179: OpenCode\nL180: \nL181:     `entire enable -y --agent opencode\nL182:     `\nL183: \nL184: Pi\nL185: \nL186:     `entire enable -y --agent pi\nL187:     `\nL188: Because this demo starts in a new folder, `entire enable -y` initializes Git, installs Git and agent hooks, creates `.entire/` settings, and makes an initial commit. If `gh` is installed and authenticated, Entire creates a private GitHub repository and pushes the commit. The initial commit includes `.entire/settings.json` and `.entire/.gitignore` so collaborators share project settings. Entire keeps `.entire/settings.local.json` local for personal overrides.\nL189: ##\nL190: \nL191: cite18†​ L192: \nL193: 4. Create Your First Checkpoint\nL194: \nL195: ###\nL196: \nL197: cite19†​ L198: \nL199: Start an Agent Session\nL200: \nL201: From the repository root, start the agent you enabled and prompt it to make a small change:\nL202: \nL203:     `Create a file named hello-entire.md with a short note explaining that this repo is my first Entire checkpoint demo.\nL204:     `\nL205: \nL206: When the agent finishes, exit the agent session if needed.\nL207: ###\nL208: \nL209: cite20†​ L210: \nL211: Commit the Change\nL212: \nL213: Check what changed:\nL214: \nL215:     `git status\nL216:     `\nL217: \nL218: Then commit it:\nL219: \nL220:     `git add hello-entire.md\nL221:     git commit -m \"Add first Entire demo file\"\nL222:     `\nL223: Because Entire detected recent agent work, it may ask whether to link this commit to the session context. The agent name will match the integration you enabled:\nL224: \nL225:     `Entire: Active <agent> session detected\nL226:       Last prompt: Create a file named hello-entire.md with a short note...\nL227: \nL228:     Link this commit to session context?\nL229:       [Y]es / [n]o / [a]lways (remember my choice):\nL230:     `\nL231: \nL232: Press `Enter` or type `y` to link the commit. After the commit succeeds, you have created your first checkpoint.\nL233: If you choose `always`, Entire remembers that preference and links future matching commits automatically.\nL234: ##\nL235: \nL236: cite21†​ L237: \nL238: 5. View Your First Checkpoint\nL239: \nL240:     `entire checkpoint list\nL241:     `\nL242: \nL243: The IDs, date, time, and commit SHA will differ. The output follows this format:\nL244: \nL245:     `branch       main\nL246:     checkpoints  1\nL247: \nL248:     ● 01KVBJCWYA4YW6J5M9GP655HZN  \"Create a file named hello-entire.md with a short note...\"\nL249:       06-17 15:54 (abc1234) Add first Entire demo file\nL250:     `\nL251: \nL252: Checkpoint storage depends on the repository configuration. See cite22†Checkpoint Storage for storage and push behavior.\nL253: Was this page helpful?\nL254: \nL255: Yes No\nL256: \nL257: cite6†Introduction Previous cite8†Installation Next L258: \nL259: [Input: Ask a question...]\nL260: \nL261: cite23†x†x.com cite24†github†github.com cite4†discord†discord.gg cite25†linkedin†www.linkedin.com cite5†website†entire.io --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGetting Started with Codex in the Entire CLI (https://entire.io/blog/getting-started-with-codex-in-entire-cli)\nciteturn30view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn29search11\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 157\nL0: APRIL 05, 2026 · Rizèl Scarlett\nL1: # Getting Started with Codex in the Entire CLI\nL2: \nL3: Engineering\nL4: \nL5: AI-assisted development accelerates experimentation, but working quickly comes with a cost.\nL6: \nL7: Developers may have access to their code, commits, and transcripts, but the relationship between these artifacts is fragile. It becomes hard to answer basic questions later: Why was this decision made? Which prompt led to this change? What was the last known-good state before things drifted?\nL8: At Entire, we are building for that boundary. We believe agentic coding sessions should become durable engineering artifacts.\nL9: \nL10: Since the cite0†Entire CLI†docs.entire.io launched, cite1†Codex†docs.entire.io has been one of the most requested agents from our community. Now Codex is supported in Entire with the same durability model we use for other built-in agents: cite2†Checkpoints†docs.entire.io tied to Git history, rewind points you can rely on, and a transparent record of how prompts became code.\nL11: ## A Real Workflow Example\nL12: \nL13: I put the Codex integration to the test by building a cite3†handwriting-to-scrapbook demo†github.com .\nL14: I used technologies I was new to, including cite4†Pretext†github.com , a new, open source, and cite5†viral†x.com layout engine that calculates text dimensions without the overhead of DOM measurements. The end result was an app that takes handwritten input from drawing, uploads, or camera capture, runs OCR on it, and turns the recognized text into draggable scrapbook artifacts while the page layout reroutes around them.\nL15: \nL16: cite6†Image: Handwriting to scrapbook demo L17: ## Starting a Codex Session with Entire\nL18: \nL19: I started by running:\nL20: \nL21:     `$entire enable --agent codex`\nL22: \nL23: This is a one-time setup per repository. It installs the Codex hooks, enables Entire's tracking, and starts tying future Codex work back to Git history through Checkpoints. Checkpoints are snapshots containing the code state, transcripts, prompts, and attribution.\nL24: ### Automating Agent Discipline\nL25: \nL26: To make the workflow more useful, I told Codex to commit early and often.\nL27: Frequent commits make agent-driven work much easier to track, but I do not always remember to do that myself. So I pushed that responsibility to the agent by adding explicit rules to the project's `AGENTS.md` file:\nL28: \nL29:     `Important working rules for this project:\nL30: \nL31:     - Keep changes and milestones separated into distinct commits.\nL32:     - After each change or milestone, commit and push before starting the next one.\nL33:     - Do not bundle unrelated work from different prompts into the same commit.\nL34:     - If a prompt would require a major architectural deviation, stop and explain before proceeding.\nL35:     `\nL36: That kept the history more structured and made the resulting Checkpoints more useful.\nL37: ## Understanding What Changed Between Checkpoints\nL38: \nL39: Midway through polishing the app, I explored letting users bring their own OpenAI API key so they could try the demo without consuming my credits. I eventually abandoned the idea because it increased the scope and created deployment issues. Codex, however, summarized that work with a commit message that only said:\nL40: \nL41:     `Harden API key gate interactions\nL42:     `\nL43: Candidly, that message does not explain what I was trying to do, what I had already tested, or why I decided not to continue.\nL44: \nL45: The command cite7†`entire explain`†docs.entire.io fills in that missing context by letting me inspect either a commit or a checkpoint directly:\nL46: \nL47:     `$# Explain by commit\nL48:     entire explain --commit <commit-sha>\nL49: \nL50:     # Explain by checkpoint\nL51:     entire explain --checkpoint <checkpoint-id>`\nL52: When I ran `entire explain` on the Checkpoint linked to `Harden API key gate interactions`, I got this output:\nL53: \nL54:     `Checkpoint: ed9285032052\nL55:     Session: 019d473b-fa3b-7480-9725-723b0aae707f\nL56:     Created: 2026-04-01 09:19:32\nL57:     Author: Rizel Scarlett <rizel@entire.io>\nL58:     Tokens: 43721\nL59: \nL60:     Commits: (1)\nL61:       200abcf 2026-04-01 Harden API key gate interactions\nL62: \nL63:     Intent: ok i want to set this up where i can deploy this but people have to p...\nL64:     Outcome: (not generated)\nL65: \nL66:     Files: (2)\nL67:       - public/js/app.js\nL68:       - public/js/lib/api-key-gate.js\nL69: \nL70:     Transcript (checkpoint scope):\nL71:     ok i want to set this up where i can deploy this but people have to put in their open ai api key in an input form to get started..so it doesn't have to be my credits..or my API key but i also dont want to have their api key get exposed or anything\nL72: \nL73:     ---\nL74: \nL75:     so will this work if i deployed to vercel? will i need to add env vars\nL76: \nL77:     ---\nL78:     before the vercel thing..i entered my key but its not letting me submit. no error messages either.\nL79: \nL80:     ---\nL81: \nL82:     Still not working. Need to pivot\nL83:     `\nL84: That output made the checkpoint immediately legible. Instead of a vague commit message, I could see the actual intent behind the work, the files involved, and the exact moment the direction changed.\nL85: ## Reverting to a Known-Good State\nL86: \nL87: Because I was having issues with the bring-your-own-key implementation, I wanted to go back to the point right before it was introduced. The following command gave me a clean way to return to a known-good state:\nL88: \nL89:     `$entire rewind`\nL90: \nL91: The cite8†`entire rewind`†docs.entire.io command first shows a list of available Checkpoints. After selecting one, you can choose from three restore modes:\nL92:   * `Restore logs only` brings back the session context without changing your working files. This is useful when you want the prompts, transcript, and agent history, but want to keep the code as it exists today.\nL93:   * `Checkout commit` restores the code at that checkpoint in a detached state so you can inspect it, run it, or compare it with your current branch without moving your branch itself.\nL94:   * `Reset branch to this commit` moves your branch back to that checkpoint and discards later branch state. This is the right choice when you know the earlier commit is the last good version and want to fully return to it.\nL95: In my case, I chose the checkpoint from before the bring-your-own-key implementation and reset the branch to that commit, because I knew that was the last working version and did not include any of the BYOK code.\nL96: ## How Entire Integrates with Codex\nL97: \nL98: Behind that workflow is the same integration pattern we use for all built-in agents, including Codex, Claude Code, Copilot, and OpenCode. Each agent gets a small adapter that tells Entire how to:\nL99: \nL100:   * Install agent hooks for lifecycle events like session start, prompt submit, and turn end\nL101:   * Read that agent's session data\nL102:   * Translate native agent events into Entire's shared lifecycle\nL103: We were able to use that same pattern for Codex, but we still had to account for a few ways it behaves differently from our other integrations.\nL104: \nL105: Codex stores session history in `JSONL` files, requires hooks to be enabled through a feature flag during setup, and exposes details like file edits and token usage through its session logs. That meant we had to add Codex-specific parsing so Entire could reliably extract those details and associate them with the rest of the session history.\nL106: cite9†Image: Codex Adapter Animation L107: \nL108: For a deeper dive into how we integrate agents, check out our cite10†agent guide†github.com .\nL109: ## Let's Build in the Open\nL110: \nL111: Codex support in Entire is in technical preview through April 30, 2026.\nL112: \nL113: If you are already using Codex and want to help shape how Entire supports agentic workflows, we invite you to apply to the Entire Insiders program. Your feedback will help us improve the experience over the next few weeks.\nL114: \nL115:   * cite11†Apply to the Entire Insiders program†forms.gle L116:   * cite12†Join the Discord†discord.gg L117:   * cite13†Open an issue†github.com L118: cite14†AUGUST 24, 2026 · Marvin Set Up Entire Without Hunting for the Next Command A new getting started flow guides you through installing the Entire CLI, logging in, enabling checkpoints, and creating your first checkpoint, while CLI and repository improvements make agent workflows more reliable.Dispatch cite15†AUGUST 17, 2026 · Marvin Instant Repo Sync from GitHub to Any Entire Region It's now easier to sync and mirror your GitHub repository across one or more Entire regions, keeping GitHub as the source of truth while placing copies closer to agents for faster parallel clones.Dispatch cite16†AUGUST 10, 2026 · Marvin Commit Pages Now Show Agent Activity in Unified View The shining spotlight this week: commit pages on [entire.io](http://entire.io) now show agent activity at a glance, bringing checkpoints, sessions, steps, file changes, and token usage into one view, making it easier to size up the work behind a commit Dispatch L119: ## Join the rebellion\nL120: \nL121: Regular updates from the front lines. Developer news, curated content, early access, and exclusive drops for people who got here first.\nL122: \nL123: cite17†Join the rebellion L124: \nL125: ### Product\nL126: \nL127:   * cite18†CLI L128:   * cite19†Documentation†docs.entire.io L129: \nL130: ### Company\nL131: \nL132:   * cite20†Blog L133:   * cite21†Vision L134:   * cite22†Company L135:   * cite23†Newsroom L136:   * cite24†Brand L137:   * cite17†Rebellion L138: \nL139: ### Legal\nL140: \nL141:   * cite25†Privacy Policy L142:   * cite26†Cookie Policy L143:   * cite27†Terms of Service L144: ### Connect\nL145: \nL146:   * cite28†GitHub†github.com L147:   * cite12†Discord†discord.gg L148:   * cite29†X†x.com L149:   * cite30†Instagram†www.instagram.com L150:   * cite31†TikTok†www.tiktok.com L151:   * cite32†LinkedIn†linkedin.com L152:   * cite33†YouTube†youtube.com L153: \nL154: Ask about Entire on\nL155: \nL156: © 2026 Entire Inc.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a04e66-c772-7170-b7f2-4f6daa7e2bfb","create_time":1788031309.787211}}}
