{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T19:22:30.876Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a04ef9-385c-7470-9a72-4cfd7558d439","call_id":"call_gRmAt8uR0Esgwpe0adPgNbSE","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 5.9 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Internal Error ()\nciteturn32view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.entire.io/guides/checkpoints/checkpoint-storage\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: URL https://docs.entire.io/guides/checkpoints/checkpoint-storage is not safe to open (non-retryable error)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn32view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.entire.io/guides/configuration/entire-files\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: URL https://docs.entire.io/guides/configuration/entire-files is not safe to open (non-retryable error)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCodex - Entire (https://docs.entire.io/agents/codex)\nciteturn32view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.entire.io/agents/codex\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 90\nL0: > ## Documentation Index\nL1: >\nL2: > Fetch the complete documentation index at: cite0†/llms.txt L3: >\nL4: > Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\nL5: \nL6: cite1†Skip to main content L7: \nL8: cite2†Entire home page L9: \nL10: Search...\nL11: \nL12: Navigation\nL13: \nL14: Supported Agents\nL15: \nL16: Codex\nL17: \nL18:   * cite3†GitHub†github.com L19:   * cite4†Discord†discord.gg L20:   * cite5†Blog†entire.io L21: ### Supported Agents\nL22: \nL23:   * cite6†Agent Integrations L24:   * cite7†Set Up an Agent L25:   * cite8†Claude Code L26:   * cite9†Codex L27:   * cite10†Copilot CLI L28:   * cite11†Cursor L29:   * cite12†Factory L30:   * cite13†Gemini CLI L31:   * cite14†OpenCode L32:   * cite15†Pi L33: \nL34: ### Agent Integration Protocol\nL35: \nL36:   * cite16†Agent Integration Protocol L37:   * cite17†Build an Agent Integration L38:   * cite18†Architecture L39:   * cite19†Lifecycle L40:   * cite20†Commands L41:   * cite21†Data Model L42: \nL43: Supported Agents\nL44: # Codex\nL45: \nL46: Capture Codex transcripts, file changes, tokens, and subagent sessions with Entire\nL47: \nL48: Copy page Copy page\nL49: \nL50: The Codex integration uses Codex’s project-level hook system and is in preview. Follow cite7†Set Up an Agent for shared installation, verification, and troubleshooting steps.\nL51: ##\nL52: \nL53: cite22†​ L54: \nL55: Add Codex\nL56: \nL57:     `entire agent add codex\nL58:     codex\nL59:     `\nL60: \nL61: Entire writes `.codex/hooks.json`. Codex enables hooks by default beginning with version 0.124.0, so Entire does not create `.codex/config.toml` or set `codex_hooks = true`. The hooks capture session boundaries, prompts, completed turns, file-changing tool calls, and subagent events. Entire reads the full conversation and token usage from Codex rollout files under `~/.codex/` or `CODEX_HOME`.\nL62: ##\nL63: \nL64: cite23†​ L65: \nL66: Limitations\nL67: \nL68: Ephemeral Codex runs may not provide a transcript path. Entire can process their hook events, but it cannot attach a full transcript without the rollout file.\nL69: ##\nL70: \nL71: cite24†​ L72: \nL73: Resume and Import\nL74: \nL75: `entire session resume BRANCH` restores the recorded session data when needed and prints:\nL76: \nL77:     `codex resume SESSION_ID\nL78:     `\nL79: \nL80: Entire can import existing Codex transcripts from the previous 30 days as read-only checkpoints. See the cite25†`session resume` command reference for flags and additional behavior.\nL81: \nL82: Was this page helpful?\nL83: \nL84: Yes No\nL85: \nL86: cite8†Claude Code Previous cite10†Copilot CLI Next L87: \nL88: [Input: Ask a question...]\nL89: cite26†x†x.com cite27†github†github.com cite4†discord†discord.gg cite28†linkedin†www.linkedin.com cite5†website†entire.io --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSecurity & Privacy - Entire (https://docs.entire.io/security)\nciteturn32view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.entire.io/security\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 313\nL0: > ## Documentation Index\nL1: >\nL2: > Fetch the complete documentation index at: cite0†/llms.txt L3: >\nL4: > Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\nL5: \nL6: cite1†Skip to main content L7: \nL8: cite2†Entire home page L9: \nL10: Search...\nL11: \nL12: Navigation\nL13: \nL14: Security\nL15: \nL16: Security & Privacy\nL17: \nL18:   * cite3†GitHub†github.com L19:   * cite4†Discord†discord.gg L20:   * cite5†Blog†entire.io L21: \nL22: ### Getting Started\nL23: \nL24:   * cite2†Overview L25:   * cite6†Introduction L26:   * cite7†Quickstart L27:   * cite8†Installation L28:   * cite9†entire.io L29: ### Guides\nL30: \nL31:   * Checkpoints\nL32: \nL33:   * Repositories\nL34: \nL35:   * Sessions\nL36: \nL37:   * entire-graph\nL38: \nL39:   * Search\nL40: \nL41:   * Dispatches\nL42: \nL43:   * Configuration\nL44: \nL45: ### Security\nL46: \nL47:   * cite10†Security & Privacy L48: \nL49: ### Resources\nL50: \nL51:   * cite11†FAQ L52:   * cite12†Glossary L53:   * cite13†Delete Your Account L54: \nL55: ## On this page\nL56: \nL57:   * cite14†Where Your Data Lives L58:   * cite15†Reporting a Vulnerability L59: \nL60: Security\nL61: # Security & Privacy\nL62: \nL63: Where your data lives, which redaction layers run before each write, when data leaves your machine, and how to report security issues\nL64: \nL65: Copy page Copy page\nL66: \nL67: Entire writes AI session transcripts and checkpoint metadata into your own Git repository, and redacts them before each write. This page covers the trust boundaries, every location Entire writes to or transmits, and which redaction layers cover each one.\nL68: We verified the behavior on this page against Entire CLI 0.10.2, except for secret scanner selection, which first appears in `v0.10.3-nightly.202608210613.680d64782`. Run `entire version` to check what you have installed. To configure any of it, see cite16†Privacy and Redaction and cite17†Checkpoint Storage ; this page describes behavior, those pages hold the settings keys.\nL69: ##\nL70: \nL71: cite14†​ L72: \nL73: Where Your Data Lives\nL74: \nL75: Checkpoints live in your Git repository, not in a service Entire controls. Which Git objects hold them depends on the storage backend.\nL76: Location  | Holds  | Scope\nL77: --- | --- | ---\nL78: `refs/entire/checkpoints/<shard>/<id>`  | One ref per checkpoint, carrying that checkpoint’s redacted metadata and transcripts. The default for repositories enabled with CLI 0.10.0 or later  | Local until you push\nL79: `entire/checkpoints/v1`  | A single branch carrying every checkpoint, used by repositories enabled before CLI 0.10.0 and by anyone who REDACTEDes `--checkpoint-backend branch`  | Local until you push\nL80: `entire/<commit>-<worktree>`  | Shadow branches: working storage during a live session, including raw, unredacted snapshots of your working tree  | Local. Entire does not push these\nL81: `.entire/`  | Settings, redaction rule packs, diagnostic logs, and local runtime state  | Local. Most of it is gitignored\nL82: Your own commits  | An `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer naming the checkpoint ID  | Public once you push\nL83: Both backends store checkpoint data in the same repository as your code unless you configure a cite18†separate checkpoint repository . To see or change which backend a repository uses, read `checkpoints.primary.type` in `.entire/settings.json` or REDACTED `--checkpoint-backend` to cite19†`entire configure` . Anyone with access to your repository can read the checkpoints in it. If your repository is public, so are they.\nL84: Treat redaction as a safety net rather than a guarantee. For customer data, internal credentials, or regulated material, use a private repository.\nL85: ##\nL86: \nL87: cite15†​ L88: \nL89: Reporting a Vulnerability\nL90: \nL91: Please do not open a public GitHub issue for a security vulnerability.\nL92: ## Email cite20†[email protected] L93: \nL94: Report security vulnerabilities directly. We acknowledge within 48 hours and aim to resolve criticals within 90 days. Full disclosure policy below.\nL95: \nL96: Threat Model and Boundaries\nL97: Entire is a local development tool that writes to your repository. The protections here assume an attacker who reads your repository, not one who already controls your machine.What redaction is for. It reduces the chance that a credential or a piece of personal data that entered an agent conversation gets committed and pushed. It runs on transcript and metadata content before Entire writes it to a Git object.What redaction does not do:\nL98:   * It does not protect a repository from the people who can read it. Anyone with read access reads every checkpoint in it.\nL99:   * It does not scan your source code. Redaction covers what Entire writes, not what you commit. A hardcoded secret in a tracked file reaches the remote through your own commit.\nL100:   * It does not defend against a compromised machine. An attacker running code as you can read the raw agent transcripts, your Git credentials, and everything under `.entire/` before redaction runs.\nL101:   * It does not catch every secret. Detection is pattern- and entropy-based. See Limitations of Redaction below.\nL102: Who can read what:\nL103: Party  | Reads  | Through\nL104: --- | --- | ---\nL105: Anyone with read access to your repository  | Redacted checkpoints, your code, and commit trailers  | Normal Git access\nL106: Anyone with read access to the checkpoint repository  | Redacted checkpoints only, when you configure a separate cite18†checkpoint remote | Normal Git access\nL107: entire.io  | The repositories you authorize, and the checkpoints in them  | The cite21†Entire GitHub App , which reads the same redacted content anyone else with repository access reads\nL108: Your summary provider  | The compacted transcript for one checkpoint, redacted first, and only when you run `entire checkpoint explain --generate`  | An outbound call to the configured provider\nL109: PostHog  | Command names, flag names, and a hashed machine ID, unless you opt out  | Anonymous CLI analytics\nL110: Entire does not operate a service that receives your transcripts. Checkpoints travel to the Git remotes you push to.\nL111: \nL112: Data Storage Details\nL113: Checkpoint storage holds metadata, not source code. A checkpoint carries `prompt.txt`, `full.jsonl`, `transcript.jsonl`, `metadata.json`, `checkpoint.json`, and an optional `assets/` subtree. Metadata records the paths a session touched, in `files_touched`, and not their contents.Shadow branches hold unredacted source. Shadow branches are the one place Entire writes your source files rather than metadata.\nL114: Metadata written to a shadow branch goes through the same redaction pipeline as a committed checkpoint, but code-file snapshots are raw blobs of your working tree, so a hardcoded secret in a tracked file appears in them verbatim. Gitignored files such as `.env` are filtered out of those snapshots as a partial defense. Entire does not push shadow branches and deletes them after a successful push once the session ends cleanly. Do not push one manually.Two redaction REDACTEDes for metadata.\nL115: Entire redacts metadata on the shadow-branch write and again at condensation time, when the session rolls into checkpoint storage. The metadata that lands in checkpoint storage, local or pushed, has been through redaction.`RedactedBytes` type guarantee. The redact package returns a typed wrapper, and the checkpoint stores accept only that type. A code path that tried to write unredacted transcript bytes would fail to compile rather than fail at runtime.Commit trailers.\nL116: Entire adds one trailer to your own commits:\nL117: \nL118:     `Entire-Checkpoint: 01J9ZQ8F7K3M2N5P6R8T9V0W1X\nL119:     `\nL120: The value is a checkpoint ID, either a 12-character hex string or a 26-character ULID. It carries no prompt content and no file content. A ULID encodes the millisecond Entire created the checkpoint, so the trailer discloses commit timing to anyone who can read the commit message. The trailer stays in your history after you push, and you can delete the line before committing if you do not want the commit linked.Remote URL redaction in logs.\nL121: When a fetch or push fails, error messages and `.entire/logs/` entries strip REDACTEDwords from URLs, for example `REDACTED`, so credentials stay out of local log files.\nL122: What Redaction Covers\nL123: \nL124: Redaction covers the content Entire writes into checkpoint storage. It does not cover everything Entire touches.\nL125: Content  | Written to  | Redaction applied  | Reaches a remote\nL126: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL127: Prompts and transcripts (`prompt.txt`, `full.jsonl`, `transcript.jsonl`)  | Checkpoint storage  | Layers 1 through 8, plus the OpenAI Privacy Filter at push time on the branch backend  | Yes, on push\nL128: Checkpoint metadata (`metadata.json`, `checkpoint.json`)  | Checkpoint storage  | Layers 1 through 8. The structural-field skip rules exempt file paths and ID fields, so `files_touched` paths are stored as written  | Yes, on push\nL129: Externalized image assets (`assets/`)  | Checkpoint storage  | None. Every layer skips the assets subtree  | Yes, on push, when `redaction.externalize_images` is on\nL130: Inline base64 images left in a transcript  | Checkpoint storage  | None. The JSONL skip rules exclude objects whose `type` starts with `image` or equals `base64`  | Yes, on push\nL131: Source-file snapshots  | Shadow branch  | None. Raw working-tree blobs, minus gitignored paths  | No, unless you push the shadow branch yourself\nL132: Raw agent session files, for example `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/*.jsonl`  | Your agent’s own directory  | None. Entire reads these files and does not rewrite them, because the agent is writing to them concurrently  | No\nL133: Diagnostic logs (`.entire/logs/entire.log`)  | Local, gitignored  | Not redacted, but the CLI logs metadata only (IDs, ref names, counts, and timings) and strips REDACTEDwords from remote URLs  | No\nL134: `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer  | Your own commits  | Not applicable. The value is an ID  | Yes, on push\nL135: Redaction Layers\nL136: \nL137: Nine layers run in this order. Layers 1 through 8 use regexes, entropy scoring, or structural validation during condensation. Layer 9 calls a local model at push time.\nL138: \nL139: CLI 0.10.2 uses Betterleaks for layer 2. Scanner selection first appears in `v0.10.3-nightly.202608210613.680d64782`.\nL140: #  | Layer  | Runs  | Replacement\nL141: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL142: 1  | Entropy scoring. Alphanumeric runs of 10 or more characters whose Shannon entropy exceeds 4.5  | On every write  | `REDACTED`\nL143: 2  | Secret pattern matching. Entire runs one or both configured scanner engines: cite22†Betterleaks†github.com , which covers several hundred known secret formats, and cite23†goredact†github.com , which validates a smaller set of provider and contextual token shapes  | On every write, with at least one scanner enabled  | `REDACTED`\nL144: 3  | Provider token prefixes. Deterministic prefix rules for low-entropy credential formats the first two layers miss in isolation, such as Supabase `sb_secret_` and `sbp_`  | On every write  | `REDACTED`\nL145: 4  | Credentialed URIs. URLs embedding a REDACTEDword, such as `REDACTED` or `REDACTED`, redacted as a unit  | On every write  | `REDACTED`\nL146: 5  | Database connection strings. JDBC URIs, database URLs, Postgres keyword DSNs, and semicolon-delimited connection strings. A string with a real REDACTEDword is redacted whole, because a partial fragment can still expose sensitive material  | On every write  | `REDACTED`\nL147: 6  | User-defined rules. Your inline rules and rule packs  | When configured  | `REDACTED`\nL148: 7  | Bounded credential values. Assignments whose key names a credential, such as `DB_PASSWORD` or `PGPASSWORD`. Inside JSON, a generic `REDACTEDword` key is redacted when the surrounding object also carries a host-like and a user-like field. The key survives; the value does not  | On every write  | `REDACTED`\nL149: 8  | PII detection. Email, phone, and US street address  | When configured  | `[REDACTED_EMAIL]` and similar\nL150: 9  | OpenAI Privacy Filter. A token-classification model that catches names and other personal data regex misses  | When configured, at push time, on the branch backend  | Category tokens\nL151: Layers 1, 3, 4, 5, and 7 run on every checkpoint write and have no setting that turns them off. Layer 2 always runs at least one scanner. Layers 6, 8, and 9 run only when you configure them.Secret scanner engines. Betterleaks runs by default and provides broader format coverage. goredact is off by default and uses validators such as token length or checksum checks to improve precision. You can run either engine or both.Entire reads scanner selection only from the committed `.entire/settings.json` file.\nL152: It ignores scanner keys in `.entire/settings.local.json` and logs a warning because scanner choice changes the checkpoint content shared with everyone who reads the repository history.Entire refuses to load settings when both scanners are off. If goredact is the only active scanner and it fails during a scan, Entire stops the checkpoint write. If Betterleaks is also active, Entire completes the write with Betterleaks coverage. See cite24†Secret Scanner Engines for configuration.Avoiding over-redaction.\nL153: Entire skips fields where a match is more likely to be noise than a secret: fields named `signature`, keys ending in `id` or `ids`, structural path fields (`filepath`, `file_path`, `cwd`, `root`, `directory`, `dir`, and `path`), and objects whose `type` starts with `image` or equals `base64`.It also leaves placeholders alone.\nL154: Entire matches placeholders exactly (`changeme`, `example`, `placeholder`, `your_REDACTEDword`, `your_secret`, and prior `REDACTED` markers) and by shape: shell expansions such as `${DB_PASSWORD}`, bracketed names such as `<REDACTEDword>`, and mask runs of three or more `*`, `x`, `.`, or `-`. A connection string whose REDACTEDword is a placeholder survives intact.These skips trade detection coverage for a lower false-positive rate. A secret parked in a `path` field or inside a base64 blob is not redacted.\nL155: User-Defined Redaction\nL156: \nL157: User-defined rules add a regex layer for strings the built-in detectors do not know: internal token formats, project codenames, customer names, or any other pattern you would rather keep out of session archives.Rules use cite25†Go RE2 syntax†pkg.go.dev , so lookarounds and backreferences are unavailable. Matches become the bare `REDACTED` token. Labels serve diagnostics only.You can supply rules two ways, both configured in cite26†Privacy and Redaction :\nL158:   * Inline rules under `redaction.custom_redactions`, for a handful of patterns.\nL159:   * Rule packs, YAML or JSON files under `.entire/redactors/`, for reusable sets. A pack declares `name` matching its filename stem, a `version`, and one or more `rules`, each with an `id` and a `regex`.\nL160: Self-tests. A rule may declare `samples` with `{ input, redacted }` expectations. Entire checks them when it loads the pack and logs a warning on a mismatch, which catches typos without disabling the rule. Entire logs an invalid regex and skips that rule rather than crashing the redactor.When rules take effect. On the next CLI or hook invocation. No reload command exists.Personal rules.\nL161: Put packs you do not want to share under `.entire/redactors/local/`, which `entire enable` keeps gitignored, so a codename or a customer reference does not become shared repository configuration.\nL162: PII Redaction (Optional)\nL163: \nL164: PII redaction runs as layer 8, in addition to secret detection. It is off by default.\nL165: \nL166: Category  | Default once PII is enabled  | Replacement\nL167: --- | --- | ---\nL168: `email`  | On  | `[REDACTED_EMAIL]`\nL169: `phone`  | On  | `[REDACTED_PHONE]`\nL170: `address` (US street addresses)  | Off, because it produces more false positives  | `[REDACTED_ADDRESS]`\nL171: Email allowlist. Bot and CI addresses stay readable, because they appear in nearly every transcript and identify no one: `noreply@*`, `actions@*`, `*@users.noreply.github.com`, and `*@noreply.github.com`.Custom patterns. You can add your own patterns under `custom_patterns`, where the label becomes part of the token. A pattern named `employee_id` produces `[REDACTED_EMPLOYEE_ID]`.A custom pattern that reveals an internal ID format is itself sensitive.\nL172: Put those in `.entire/settings.local.json`, which stays out of Git, rather than in the committed `.entire/settings.json`. See cite27†PII redaction for the settings.\nL173: OpenAI Privacy Filter (Optional)\nL174: \nL175: The cite28†OpenAI Privacy Filter†github.com (`opf`) is a 1.5B-parameter token-classification model that finds names, addresses, account numbers, and other personal data the regex layers miss. It runs as layer 9, and it is off by default.\nL176: OPF applies to the branch backend. The pre-push rewrite operates on `entire/checkpoints/v1`. Repositories on the ref-based backend, which is the default for repositories enabled with CLI 0.10.0 or later, push checkpoints without the OPF REDACTED. Check `checkpoints.primary.type` before relying on OPF.\nL177: When it runs. OPF runs at push time, not at commit time. Local commits stay on the eight regex layers, so per-commit latency is unchanged. During `git push`, the pre-push hook re-reads each unpushed checkpoint commit, runs the model over its content, and writes new commits carrying an `Entire-OPF-Applied: true` trailer. The local ref fast-forwards to the rewritten tip, and those commits are what reach the remote. The original commits become unreachable and are swept by `git gc`.Two consequences follow.\nL178: The remote sees only OPF-redacted content once OPF is enabled, and local-only commits carry eight-layer redaction until the moment you push. If you never push, OPF never runs.The pre-push prompt. When OPF is enabled and your terminal is interactive, the hook asks before running:\nL179: \nL180:     `Run OpenAI Privacy Filter on these checkpoints?\nL181:     Adds ~30s but redacts names/PII the regex layers can't catch.\nL182:     Ctrl-C to cancel the push.\nL183: \nL184:       > Yes     run OPF this push\nL185:         No      skip OPF, push as-is\nL186:         Always  run OPF on every push from now on\nL187:     `\nL188: Yes runs OPF for this push. No skips it, and the eight-layer content reaches the remote. Always runs OPF and writes `prompt_default: \"always\"` to `.entire/settings.local.json` so later pushes stop asking. Ctrl-C aborts the push, and no refs reach the remote.Without a TTY, which covers CI and scripted pushes, Entire skips the prompt and runs OPF, printing a progress line to stderr so the wait is visible.The `ENTIRE_OPF` override. `ENTIRE_OPF` beats both the setting and the prompt, for a single push.\nL189: The decision resolves in this order: `ENTIRE_OPF`, then `prompt_default` in settings, then the prompt, then the non-interactive default of running.\nL190: \nL191:     `ENTIRE_OPF=no git push\nL192:     `\nL193: `ENTIRE_OPF=no` skips OPF for that push. `ENTIRE_OPF=yes` runs it without asking. Use `no` when CI pushes from a machine with no `opf` installed, or for a one-off push while you fix a broken install.Failures abort the push. If `opf` is missing from `$PATH`, fails to start, or times out, the rewrite aborts the push with `OPF runtime failed; aborting push`. Nothing reaches the remote.\nL194: Enabling OPF states that you do not want unredacted content leaving the machine, so falling back to eight-layer redaction silently would break that contract.Enabling OPF with no category selected fails the same way. Entire cannot tag commits as OPF-applied without a scan, so the push aborts with a configuration error instead. Enable a category, set `enabled: false`, or REDACTED `ENTIRE_OPF=no`.The rewrite also refuses to run when it would rewrite history it does not own.\nL195: It aborts if local checkpoints have diverged from the remote, if a first push exceeds the bootstrap commit cap, if the batch exceeds the inference byte cap, or if another worktree moves the ref mid-rewrite. See cite29†OpenAI Privacy Filter for the caps and their overrides.`command` is local-only, and the file must be untracked. `command` names the binary Entire executes during `git push`, so whoever controls that string controls what runs on your machine. Entire honors it only when it is genuinely yours:\nL196:   1. It has to come from `.entire/settings.local.json`, not the committed `.entire/settings.json`.\nL197:   2. That file has to be untracked, meaning absent from both the Git index and `HEAD`.\nL198: The second check carries the weight. A filename proves nothing on its own, because `.gitignore` does not apply to an already-tracked path: `git add -f .entire/settings.local.json` commits the file, and a fresh clone materializes it with the committed content. A pull request that pairs a `command` with a payload would then execute on every machine that checks the branch out, and a JSON settings diff does not read as executable to a reviewer.The rule covers the whole file.\nL199: A tracked `.entire/settings.local.json` is ignored entirely, because it arrived with the repository rather than belonging to your clone, and Entire tells you to run `git rm --cached .entire/settings.local.json`. Settings still load from the project file, so a committed local-settings file cannot break the repository.Entire logs a rejected `command` to `.entire/logs/entire.log`, and OPF falls back to resolving `opf` on `$PATH`.\nL200: If that binary is missing, the push fails closed rather than shipping content you believed OPF had scanned. Everything else in the OPF block, including `enabled`, `categories`, `timeout_seconds`, and `prompt_default`, works from the shared project file.Content OPF did not reach. Several local locations retain content that predates the OPF REDACTED. This matters if your threat model extends past what reaches the remote.\nL201: Location  | Redaction level  | Lifetime\nL202: --- | --- | ---\nL203: Raw agent session files  | None  | Until your agent deletes them\nL204: Shadow branches  | Eight layers  | Deleted after the next successful push, once the session ends cleanly\nL205: Unreachable objects from the rewrite  | Eight layers  | Until `git gc --prune`, which defaults to two weeks\nL206: Reflog for the checkpoint ref  | Eight-layer tips  | `gc.reflogExpire`, which defaults to 90 days\nL207: To clear the unreachable objects sooner:\nL208: \nL209:     `git reflog expire --expire-unreachable=now refs/heads/entire/checkpoints/v1 && git gc --prune=now\nL210:     `\nL211: \nL212: This is I/O-heavy on a large repository, so Entire does not run it for you.\nL213: \nL214: Externalized Images\nL215: Agents inline base64 images into their transcripts. Redaction skips those objects, so an image reaches checkpoint storage as-is either way. What `redaction.externalize_images` changes is where the bytes sit.With externalization on, Entire lifts each inline image out of the transcript into the checkpoint’s `assets/` store and leaves a placeholder in its place:\nL216: \nL217:     `entire-asset:assets/img-4f3a2b1c9d8e7f60.png\nL218:     `\nL219: The placeholder is deliberately low-entropy so the entropy layer does not flag it, and it names the asset path so an agent reading the stored transcript still knows an image was there. `assets/manifest.json` indexes the store. Restore re-injects the images byte-exactly whether or not the flag is on, and Entire refuses to externalize any image whose bytes do not re-encode to the original base64 string.Nothing redacts the assets subtree.\nL220: Every regex layer skips it, and the OPF rewrite copies it through verbatim, because byte-redacting an image would corrupt it. An image holding a credential, a customer record, or a screenshot of a dashboard reaches the remote intact, whether it sits inline in the transcript or in `assets/`.Externalization is off by default. Turning it on keeps large binary blobs out of the transcript files, which makes transcripts cheaper to read and diff. It does not add or remove any redaction coverage.\nL221: See cite30†Externalized images for the setting.\nL222: Limitations of Redaction\nL223:   * Detection is best-effort. Short REDACTEDwords and predictable tokens carry too little entropy and match no known pattern.\nL224:   * Filenames and binary data go unscanned. A secret in a filename, a binary file, or a deeply nested structure may go undetected.\nL225:   * Skip rules can hide secrets. The structural-field and image skip rules avoid false positives at the cost of coverage, so a secret in a `path` field or a base64 blob survives.\nL226:   * Assets are not scanned at all. See Externalized Images above.\nL227:   * You own your custom rules. Entire logs and skips an invalid regex in `custom_redactions`, a rule pack, or `custom_patterns`. It does not check that your rules catch what you meant them to catch.\nL228:   * Source snapshots are not covered. Custom rules and every other layer apply to transcript, prompt, and checkpoint metadata. Shadow branches still carry raw source.\nL229:   * Review what you push. Redaction reduces exposure; it does not certify a checkpoint as safe to publish.\nL230: External Services and Data Egress\nL231: \nL232: The CLI runs locally. It reaches the network in these cases:\nL233: When  | Where  | What Entire sends  | Redacted\nL234: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL235: `git push` on an Entire-enabled repository  | Your Git remote  | Redacted checkpoints, alongside your code  | Yes, before the data reached the ref\nL236: `git push` with a cite18†checkpoint remote configured  | The separate checkpoint repository  | Redacted checkpoints only  | Yes\nL237: `entire checkpoint explain --generate`  | The provider set by `summary_generation.provider`, typically the agent that ran the session  | The compacted transcript for the checkpoint being summarized  | Yes, redaction runs before the transcript reaches the provider\nL238: Telemetry, unless you opt out  | PostHog at `eu.i.posthog.com`  | Command name, agent name, flag names, CLI version, OS and architecture, Git version, and a hashed machine ID  | Not applicable. No transcript or code content is sent\nL239: GitHub authentication during setup flows that touch GitHub  | GitHub  | Delegated to the `gh` CLI. Entire stores no credentials of its own  | Not applicable\nL240: `entire plugin install` and `entire plugin search`  | The plugin’s forge and the plugin index  | Nothing beyond the request itself  | Not applicable\nL241: Entire pushes checkpoint data only when `strategy_options.push_sessions` is true, which is the default. Set it to `false` to cite31†keep checkpoints local .\nL242: \nL243: Executables Entire Runs\nL244: Entire runs third-party binaries in two situations, and both give the binary your user’s privileges.CLI plugins. `entire <name>` runs `entire-<name>` when the built-in commands do not match. The CLI resolves the name against `$PATH`, and at startup it prepends a managed directory to `$PATH`:\nL245: \nL246:     `$ENTIRE_PLUGIN_DIR/bin           # override\nL247:     $XDG_DATA_HOME/entire/plugins/bin\nL248:     ~/.local/share/entire/plugins/bin\nL249:     `\nL250: `entire plugin install` downloads release binaries into that directory. Two properties bound the download:\nL251:   * The CLI requires HTTPS. It refuses plaintext HTTP for anything off-machine, because the asset and the `checksums.txt` that authenticates it share an origin, so an attacker who can rewrite one can rewrite the other.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a04e66-c772-7170-b7f2-4f6daa7e2bfb","create_time":1788031350.876212}}}
