{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T19:24:29.549Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_08520c685d1a1132016a9331eb07dc87d2878a9eba87e7d057","status":"completed","call_id":"call_Q7ZHZWWPXJOvkFMXOKgdSrMK","name":"exec","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"sed -n '23118,24211p' /var/folders/0g/0jg2ynjn33sftyg101714qvw0000gn/T/openai-docs-cache/codex-manual.md | rg -n -C 6 \\\"trust|approve|/hooks|project-level|project hook|hooks.json\\\" | sed -n '1,420p'\",workdir:\"/Users/julian/code/testcenter-rewrite\",yield_time_ms:10000,max_output_tokens:20000});\ntext(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a04e66-c772-7170-b7f2-4f6daa7e2bfb","create_time":1788031465.251903}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T19:24:29.674Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a04efb-086a-76f2-acd9-eade53065bf1","call_id":"call_Q7ZHZWWPXJOvkFMXOKgdSrMK","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"/Users/julian/.zprofile:source:8: no such file or directory: /Users/julian/.bashrc\n1-### Hooks\n2-\n3:Source: [Hooks](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/hooks.md)\n4-\n5-Hooks are an extensibility framework for Codex. They let you run scripts or MCP\n6-tools during the agentic loop, enabling features such as:\n7-\n8-- Send the chat to a custom logging/analytics engine\n9-- Scan your team's prompts to block accidentally pasting API keys\n--\n13-\n14-Runtime behavior to keep in mind:\n15-\n16-- Matching hooks from multiple files all run.\n17-- Multiple matching command hooks for the same event are launched concurrently,\n18-  so one hook can't prevent another matching hook from starting.\n19:- Non-managed hooks must be reviewed and trusted before they run.\n20-\n21-Hooks run at different points in a conversation:\n22-\n23-| When                              | Hooks                                                                                                                     |\n24-| --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n25-| During a turn                     | `PreToolUse`, `PermissionRequest`, `PostToolUse`, `PreCompact`, `PostCompact`, `UserPromptSubmit`, `SubagentStop`, `Stop` |\n--\n27-| When the main thread ends         | `SessionEnd` (doesn't run for subagents)                                                                                  |\n28-\n29-#### Where Codex looks for hooks\n30-\n31-Codex discovers hooks next to active config layers in either of these forms:\n32-\n33:- `hooks.json`\n34-- inline `[hooks]` tables inside `config.toml`\n35-\n36-Installed plugins can also bundle lifecycle config through their plugin\n37:manifest or a default `hooks/hooks.json` file. See [Build\n38-plugins](https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/plugins#bundled-mcp-servers-and-lifecycle-hooks) for the\n39-plugin packaging rules.\n40-\n41-In practice, the four most useful locations are:\n42-\n43:- `~/.codex/hooks.json`\n44-- `~/.codex/config.toml`\n45:- `/.codex/hooks.json`\n46-- `/.codex/config.toml`\n47-\n48-If more than one hook source exists, Codex loads all matching hooks.\n49-Higher-precedence config layers don't replace lower-precedence hooks.\n50:If a single layer contains both `hooks.json` and inline `[hooks]`, Codex\n51-merges them and warns at startup. Prefer one representation per layer.\n52-\n53-Codex can also discover hooks bundled with enabled plugins. Plugin-bundled\n54:hooks load alongside other hook sources and use the same trust-review flow as\n55-other non-managed hooks.\n56-\n57:Project-local hooks load only when the project `.codex/` layer is trusted. In\n58:untrusted projects, Codex still loads user and system hooks from their own\n59-active config layers.\n60-\n61:#### Review and trust hooks\n62-\n63-Codex lists configured hooks before deciding which ones can run. Before a\n64:non-managed hook can run, Codex requires you to review and trust the exact hook\n65:definition. Codex records trust against the hook's current hash, so new or\n66:changed hooks are marked for review and skipped until trusted.\n67-\n68:Use `/hooks` in the CLI to inspect hook sources, review new or changed hooks,\n69:trust hooks, or disable individual non-managed hooks. If hooks need review at\n70:startup, Codex prints a warning that tells you to open `/hooks`.\n71-\n72-Managed hooks from system, MDM, cloud, or `requirements.toml` sources are marked\n73:as managed, trusted by policy, and can't be disabled from the user hook browser.\n74-\n75-For one-off automation that already vets hook sources outside Codex, pass\n76:`--dangerously-bypass-hook-trust` to run enabled hooks without requiring\n77:persisted hook trust for that invocation.\n78-\n79-#### Config shape\n80-\n81-Hooks are organized in three levels:\n82-\n83-- A hook event such as `PreToolUse`, `PostToolUse`, `PreCompact`,\n--\n92-    \"SessionStart\": [\n93-      {\n94-        \"matcher\": \"startup|resume\",\n95-        \"hooks\": [\n96-          {\n97-            \"type\": \"command\",\n98:            \"command\": \"python3 ~/.codex/hooks/session_start.py\",\n99-            \"statusMessage\": \"Loading session notes\",\n100-            \"additionalContextLimit\": 5000\n101-          }\n102-        ]\n103-      }\n104-    ],\n105-    \"SessionEnd\": [\n106-      {\n107-        \"hooks\": [\n108-          {\n109-            \"type\": \"command\",\n110:            \"command\": \"python3 ~/.codex/hooks/session_end.py\",\n111-            \"timeout\": 3\n112-          }\n113-        ]\n114-      }\n115-    ],\n116-    \"PreToolUse\": [\n117-      {\n118-        \"matcher\": \"Bash\",\n119-        \"hooks\": [\n120-          {\n121-            \"type\": \"command\",\n122:            \"command\": \"/usr/bin/python3 \\\"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/.codex/hooks/pre_tool_use_policy.py\\\"\",\n123-            \"statusMessage\": \"Checking Bash command\"\n124-          }\n125-        ]\n126-      }\n127-    ],\n128-    \"PermissionRequest\": [\n129-      {\n130-        \"matcher\": \"Bash\",\n131-        \"hooks\": [\n132-          {\n133-            \"type\": \"command\",\n134:            \"command\": \"/usr/bin/python3 \\\"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/.codex/hooks/permission_request.py\\\"\",\n135-            \"statusMessage\": \"Checking approval request\"\n136-          }\n137-        ]\n138-      }\n139-    ],\n140-    \"PostToolUse\": [\n141-      {\n142-        \"matcher\": \"Bash\",\n143-        \"hooks\": [\n144-          {\n145-            \"type\": \"command\",\n146:            \"command\": \"/usr/bin/python3 \\\"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/.codex/hooks/post_tool_use_review.py\\\"\",\n147-            \"statusMessage\": \"Reviewing Bash output\"\n148-          }\n149-        ]\n150-      }\n151-    ],\n152-    \"UserPromptSubmit\": [\n153-      {\n154-        \"hooks\": [\n155-          {\n156-            \"type\": \"command\",\n157:            \"command\": \"/usr/bin/python3 \\\"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/.codex/hooks/user_prompt_submit_data_flywheel.py\\\"\"\n158-          }\n159-        ]\n160-      }\n161-    ],\n162-    \"Stop\": [\n163-      {\n164-        \"hooks\": [\n165-          {\n166-            \"type\": \"command\",\n167:            \"command\": \"/usr/bin/python3 \\\"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/.codex/hooks/stop_continue.py\\\"\",\n168-            \"timeout\": 30\n169-          }\n170-        ]\n171-      }\n172-    ]\n173-  }\n174-}\n175-```\n176-\n177-Notes:\n178-\n179:- `description` is optional top-level metadata for a `hooks.json` file. It\n180-  doesn't change which hooks run.\n181-- `timeout` is in seconds.\n182-- If `timeout` is omitted, Codex uses `600` seconds for most hooks.\n183-  - `SessionEnd` uses `1` second by default and supports up to `3` seconds.\n184-- `statusMessage` is optional.\n185-- `additionalContextLimit` sets how much `additionalContext` a command hook can\n--\n190-- Set `async` to `true` to [run a command hook in the\n191-  background](#run-hooks-in-the-background).\n192-- `command` and `mcp_tool` handlers are supported. `prompt` and `agent`\n193-  handlers are parsed but skipped.\n194-- Commands run with the session `cwd` as their working directory.\n195-- For repo-local hooks, prefer resolving from the git root instead of using a\n196:  relative path such as `.codex/hooks/...`. Codex may be started from a\n197-  subdirectory, and a git-root-based path keeps the hook location stable.\n198-\n199-Equivalent inline TOML in `config.toml`:\n200-\n201-```toml\n202-[[hooks.SessionStart]]\n203-matcher = \"^compact$\"\n204-\n205-[[hooks.SessionStart.hooks]]\n206-type = \"command\"\n207:command = '/usr/bin/python3 \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/.codex/hooks/session_start.py\"'\n208-additionalContextLimit = 5000\n209-\n210-[[hooks.PreToolUse]]\n211-matcher = \"^Bash$\"\n212-\n213-[[hooks.PreToolUse.hooks]]\n214-type = \"command\"\n215:command = '/usr/bin/python3 \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/.codex/hooks/pre_tool_use_policy.py\"'\n216-timeout = 30\n217-statusMessage = \"Checking Bash command\"\n218-\n219-[[hooks.PostToolUse]]\n220-matcher = \"^Bash$\"\n221-\n222-[[hooks.PostToolUse.hooks]]\n223-type = \"command\"\n224:command = '/usr/bin/python3 \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/.codex/hooks/post_tool_use_review.py\"'\n225-timeout = 30\n226-statusMessage = \"Reviewing Bash output\"\n227-```\n228-\n229-#### MCP tool hooks\n230-\n231-An MCP tool hook lets a lifecycle event call a tool on an already-connected MCP\n232-server. It sends structured arguments directly to the tool and uses the same\n233:trust review and output contract as a command hook.\n234-\n235-#### Configure an MCP tool hook\n236-\n237-This hook asks the `scanner` MCP server to scan each patch after Codex writes or\n238-edits files:\n239-\n--\n335-allow_managed_hooks_only = true\n336-\n337-[features]\n338-hooks = true\n339-\n340-[hooks]\n341:managed_dir = \"/enterprise/hooks\"\n342-windows_managed_dir = 'C:\\enterprise\\hooks'\n343-\n344-[[hooks.PreToolUse]]\n345-matcher = \"^Bash$\"\n346-\n347-[[hooks.PreToolUse.hooks]]\n348-type = \"command\"\n349:command = \"python3 /enterprise/hooks/pre_tool_use_policy.py\"\n350-command_windows = 'py -3 C:\\enterprise\\hooks\\pre_tool_use_policy.py'\n351-timeout = 30\n352-statusMessage = \"Checking managed Bash command\"\n353-```\n354-\n355-Notes for managed hooks:\n--\n366-\n367-#### Plugin-bundled hooks\n368-\n369-When a plugin is enabled, Codex can load lifecycle hooks from that plugin\n370-alongside user, project, and managed hooks.\n371-\n372:By default, Codex looks for `hooks/hooks.json` inside the plugin root. A plugin\n373-manifest can override that default with a `hooks` entry in\n374-`.codex-plugin/plugin.json`. The manifest entry can be a `./`-prefixed path, an\n375-array of `./`-prefixed paths, an inline hooks object, or an array of inline\n376-hooks objects.\n377-\n378-```json\n379-{\n380-  \"name\": \"repo-policy\",\n381:  \"hooks\": \"./hooks/hooks.json\"\n382-}\n383-```\n384-\n385-Manifest hook paths are resolved relative to the plugin root and must stay\n386-inside that root. If a manifest defines `hooks`, Codex uses those manifest\n387:entries instead of the default `hooks/hooks.json`.\n388-\n389-Plugin hook commands receive these environment variables:\n390-\n391-- `PLUGIN_ROOT` is a Codex-specific extension that points to the installed\n392-  plugin root.\n393-- `PLUGIN_DATA` is a Codex-specific extension that points to the plugin's\n394-  writable data directory.\n395-- Codex also sets `CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT` and `CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA` for\n396-  compatibility with existing plugin hooks.\n397-\n398-Plugin hooks use the same event schema as other hooks. Installing or enabling a\n399:plugin doesn't automatically trust its hooks; Codex skips plugin-bundled hooks\n400:until you review and trust the current hook definition.\n401-\n402-#### Matcher patterns\n403-\n404-The `matcher` field is a regex string that filters when hooks fire. Use `\"*\"`,\n405-`\"\"`, or omit `matcher` entirely to match every occurrence of a supported\n406-event.\n--\n535-`additionalContextLimit` on the handler to customize the approximate token\n536-threshold:\n537-\n538-```json\n539-{\n540-  \"type\": \"command\",\n541:  \"command\": \"python3 ~/.codex/hooks/session_start.py\",\n542-  \"additionalContextLimit\": 5000\n543-}\n544-```\n545-\n546-Omit `additionalContextLimit` to use the default `2500`-token threshold. Use a\n547-positive integer to select a different threshold, or `0` to pass the handler's\n--\n561-By default, Codex waits for a command hook to finish before continuing the\n562-operation that triggered it. Set `async` to `true` to run a command hook in the\n563-background while Codex continues.\n564-\n565-#### Configure a background hook\n566-\n567:Add `\"async\": true` to a command handler in `hooks.json`:\n568-\n569-```json\n570-{\n571-  \"hooks\": {\n572-    \"PostToolUse\": [\n573-      {\n574-        \"matcher\": \"Bash\",\n575-        \"hooks\": [\n576-          {\n577-            \"type\": \"command\",\n578:            \"command\": \"python3 ~/.codex/hooks/post_tool_use.py\",\n579-            \"async\": true,\n580-            \"timeout\": 120\n581-          }\n582-        ]\n583-      }\n584-    ]\n--\n591-```toml\n592-[[hooks.PostToolUse]]\n593-matcher = \"Bash\"\n594-\n595-[[hooks.PostToolUse.hooks]]\n596-type = \"command\"\n597:command = \"python3 ~/.codex/hooks/post_tool_use.py\"\n598-async = true\n599-timeout = 120\n600-```\n601-\n602:Background hooks use the same input, matcher, trust review, timeout, and\n603-[large-output handling](#large-hook-output) as synchronous command hooks. As\n604-with other command hooks, `timeout` is measured in seconds and defaults to\n605-`600`.\n606-\n607-#### How background hooks run\n608-\n--\n616-  background hook doesn't start a new turn.\n617-\n618-Use the same event-specific JSON output as a synchronous hook. Codex adds\n619-`additionalContext` to the model's context and surfaces `systemMessage` as a\n620-warning.\n621-\n622:Background hooks can't block, approve, rewrite, or otherwise control the\n623-operation that triggered them. Use synchronous hooks for tool policies,\n624-permission decisions, prompt rejection, or turn continuation.\n625-\n626-#### Limitations\n627-\n628-- Codex runs up to eight background hooks concurrently per session. Additional\n--\n808-For Bash commands and `apply_patch`, `updatedInput` must include a string\n809-`command` field. For MCP and other local function tools, `updatedInput` is the\n810-replacement arguments object. Return `updatedInput` only with\n811-`permissionDecision: \"allow\"`; other `updatedInput` shapes are reported as\n812-errors.\n813-\n814:`permissionDecision: \"ask\"`, legacy `decision: \"approve\"`, `continue: false`,\n815-`stopReason`, and `suppressOutput` are parsed but not supported yet. Codex marks\n816-the hook run as failed, reports the error, and continues the tool call.\n817-\n818-#### PermissionRequest\n819-\n820-`PermissionRequest` runs when Codex is about to ask for approval, such as a\n--\n837-\n838-Plain text on `stdout` is ignored.\n839-\n840-Some tool inputs may include a human-readable description, but don't rely on a\n841-`tool_input.description` field for every tool.\n842-\n843:To approve the request, return:\n844-\n845-```json\n846-{\n847-  \"hookSpecificOutput\": {\n848-    \"hookEventName\": \"PermissionRequest\",\n849-    \"decision\": {\n--\n1087-#### Schemas\n1088-\n1089-The linked `main` branch schemas may include hook fields that are not in the\n1090-current release. Use this page as the release behavior reference.\n1091-\n1092-If you need the exact current wire format, see the generated schemas in the\n1093:[Codex GitHub repository](https://github.com/openai/codex/tree/main/codex-rs/hooks/schema/generated).\n1094-\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a04e66-c772-7170-b7f2-4f6daa7e2bfb","create_time":1788031469.674642}}}
